   "body_extracted": "Internet restricted after fighting breaks out in Ethiopia's Tigray region\nImage source,  AFP via Getty Images\nImage caption,    The internet was cut in Tigray during the two-year civil war which ended in 2022 (file photo)\nBy   Kaleb Moges   ,  BBC Africa      and    Ondiro Oganga\nPublished\n25 September 2026\nInternet and call services have been disrupted in Ethiopia's northern region of Tigray following the outbreak of fighting this week between regional forces and the national army.\nServices provided by state-owned telecoms operator Ethio Telecom have been completely cut, although those from the smaller private firm Safaricom remain operational. Internet monitoring group NetBlocks earlier posted on X that network data indicated a sub-national internet shutdown in northern Tigray.\n\"Last night telecommunications got knocked out,\" Duke Burbridge, an American humanitarian worker in Tigray's capital, Mekelle, told the BBC's Newsday programme.\nThe BBC has contacted Ethiopia's authorities for comment.\nSources have told the Reuters news agency that the group which controls Tigray, the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), ordered the communications blackout, with one suggesting it was to disrupt government drones.\nThe TPLF has not commented.\nThe BBC is unable to report from Tigray and surrounding areas.\nEven before the current disruption, finding video and images to establish what is happening on the ground has been a challenge.\nSo far, other than footage from Mekelle, most of what has been shared online has been from outside the region.\nThe young women who fought in Ethiopia's last civil war and don't want to see another one\nPublished\n6 March\nBurbridge said there had been a scarcity of cash in Tigray for the past eight months, with people unable to access ATMs so the lack of internet services would be \"devastating for everybody\".\nMany people rely on banking and mobile money applications for transactions, with the majority using Ethio Telecom and Safaricom's networks to access these services.\nOn Thursday a Mekelle resident who we are only identifying as Fitsum made a similar point.\nHe told the BBC \"there is almost no cash\" in the city.\nWith the outbreak of war, people were limiting their movements and the city was all quiet - \"it's like a curfew has been placed,\" he said.\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser.\nThis video can not be played\nFigure caption,    Tigray capital 'out of cash' as fighting escalates\nClashes between Tigrayan forces and their allies and Ethiopia's federal troops broke out on Wednesday, prompting fears of a return to a full scale civil war and reports of panic buying.\nA humanitarian source in the neighbouring region of Afar told the AFP news agency there was \"heavy fighting overnight\", including drone strikes, around the town of Abala in the mountains between Afar and Tigray, as \"federal forces pushed the rebels from entering the town\".\nThere have also been reports of fighting near the town of Lalibela in the Amhara region, which is home to churches hewn out of rock, recognised as a UN World Heritage site. The US has warned against travel to all three regions.\nDuring the two-year conflict which ended in 2022, Tigray was totally cut off from the outside world.\nLimited access to cash, food and medicine pushed hundreds of thousands into famine-like conditions.\nIt was one of the most deadly conflicts this century - with estimates that some 600,000 people died as the fighting drove the region to the precipice of famine.\nOn Sunday, the TPLF announced an alliance with six other armed groups to oust the government.\nOn Wednesday, its forces seized control of three airports from federal police.\nFollowing the seizure of the airports, Ethiopian Airlines has cancelled all flights to Tigray.\nAdditional reporting by Peter Mwai, BBC Verify\nMore about Tigray from the BBC:\n'I lost my leg on the way home from school'\nPublished\n2 November 2024\nIs Ethiopia heading back to war in Tigray?\nPublished\n26 February\nEthiopia PM's party wins landslide as fears grow of new conflict\nPublished\n21 June\nGo to    BBCAfrica.com  ,  external    for more news from the African continent.\nFollow us on Twitter    @BBCAfrica  ,  external   , on Facebook at    BBC Africa  ,  external    or on Instagram at    bbcafrica  ,  external\nRelated topics\nEthiopia\nEthiopian civil war\nTigray People's Liberation Front\nBBC Africa podcasts\nFocus on Africa\nThis Is Africa",
