  {
   "event_id": "EVT_e3d74b3f42787da2",
   "country": "SS",
   "country_cn": "南苏丹",
   "country_risk_level": 4,
   "country_risk_label": "极高",
   "event_type": "armed_conflict",
   "event_severity": "medium",
   "event_status": "new",
   "title_cn": "上尼罗河大学将在马拉卡勒恢复学院办学",
   "title_original": "Upper Nile University set to reopen college in Malakal",
   "summary_cn": "南苏丹公立大学上尼罗河大学表示，已基本完成在马拉卡勒恢复人力资源开发学院教学的准备工作，课程计划于10月第一周开始。该学院因2013年12月爆发的冲突迁至朱巴，目前已有92名学生被录取。校方称部分宿舍被武装部队人员占用，正与州政府协商腾退，并解决水电短缺问题。",
   "summary_original": "Upper Nile University, one of South Sudan’s public universities, says it has completed most preparations The post Upper Nile University set to reopen college in Malakal appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .",
   "event_time": "2026-09-27T09:58:22Z",
   "published_time": "2026-09-27T09:58:22Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/upper-nile-university-set-to-reopen-college-in-malakal",
     "source_name": "Radio Tamazuj",
     "source_group": "ssd",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Upper Nile University, one of South Sudan’s public universities, says it has completed most preparations to resume teaching at its Human Resource Development College in Malakal, more than a decade after conflict disrupted its operations there.\nClasses are due to begin in the first week of October for the 2026-27 academic year, according to Isaac Deng Akuei, director of the university’s Human Resource Development College.\nAkuei told Radio Tamazuj on Saturday that lecture halls, textbook and teaching staff were ready at the university’s main campus in Malakal, the capital of Upper Nile State.\nThe university began admitting students for diploma courses in February. So far, 92 students have been admitted, Akuei said.\n“Human Resource Development is the college that runs and offers diplomas at the University of Upper Nile. The first batch is in Juba because the college was relocated to Juba because of the war,” he said.\nThe university was originally based in Malakal but moved operations to Juba after conflict broke out in South Sudan in December 2013.\nAkuei said plans to return the university’s operations fully to Malakal had faced difficulties, but the institution had decided to resume diploma courses there to give local students access to higher education.\n“The plan that is in place now is to ensure our sons and daughters in Malakal who want to join higher education get their education in Malakal,” he said.\nThe university is still working to secure student accommodation. Some of its dormitories are occupied by members of the organised forces, according to Akuei.\nHe said the university is working with the Upper Nile state government to have the facilities vacated and prepared for students.\nThe university is also working with other stakeholders to address shortages of water and electricity, he added.\nRegistration and orientation are due to begin on Monday. Students who missed the initial application deadline will be allowed to apply directly through the university, Akuei said.\nThe college will initially offer seven diploma courses: public administration, computer science, rural development, accounting, business administration, secretarial and office management, and family science.\nMost tuition fees will not exceed 1.5 million South Sudanese pounds, Akuei said, acknowledging the financial difficulties faced by many families.\n“We want students to take advantage of this opportunity to resume their studies at the university in the state,” he said.\nResidents welcome return\nResidents in Malakal have welcomed the move, saying it could give young people greater access to higher education after years of disruption.\nAban Kwang, a resident of Upper Nile State, said many young people had lost opportunities to pursue higher education because of the conflict.\n“Many young people lost opportunities to pursue higher education during the years of conflict,” he said.\nAnother resident, Teresa Ojok, said the return of the university could expand educational opportunities and contribute to social and economic development.",
