   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Judge imposes £100,000 bail condition on Rwanda genocide accused\nImage caption,    Dr Vincent Brown was allegedly a \"leader\" or \"supervisor\" of violent acts carried out by others\nBy  Matt Spivey\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nA man accused of offences relating to the 1994 Rwanda genocide will remain in custody unless he pays the court a £100,000 guarantee.\nDr Vincent Brown, formerly Dr Vincent Bajinya, 65, was allegedly a \"leader\" or \"supervisor\" of violent acts carried out by others and is the first person in the UK to be charged over the genocide.\nAt the Old Bailey on Monday, Judge Mark Lucraft KC granted conditional bail with stricter conditions than at a previous hearing, including the increased surety and electronic monitoring.\nBrown is accused of \"killing, directing or encouraging\" the deaths of members of the Tutsi ethnic group in Rwanda. He denies wrongdoing.\nThree members of the same house were allegedly murdered, along with a taxi driver, a mother and her young son between April 6 and 13.\nThe alleged victims were Dominique Mukurarinda, Immaculee Mukankusi, Gerasi Rurangirwa, Leandre Mbogoye, Charlotte Kamaguja and Olivier Tuyishime.\nBrown, of Islington, north London, was charged following a seven-year British investigation into the killings.\nAn estimated 800,000 Tutsis and politically moderate Hutus were killed during the 1994 Rwanda genocide which lasted for 100 days.\nOn Wednesday the judge imposed a £100,000 bail condition on Brown's release. This is up from an earlier £25,000 security imposed by magistrates.\nBrown was also ordered to surrender his passport and any national identity cards, not to apply for international travel documents and was told he must abide by a curfew to live and sleep at his London address.\nHe would also be electronically monitored and have to report daily to police.\nLucraft said that until the £100,000 condition was met, the defendant would remain in custody.\nBrown is a medical doctor who qualified in Rwanda in the 1980s. He later became head of the country's Department of Family Health in Kigali and lived in a suburb there with his wife and young son.\nIn 2006 he was worked at Praxis, a London-based charity which helps refugees from places like Rwanda.\nHe has also served on the Refugee Nurses Task Force, which was set up in 2003 to advise the UK government.\nBrown is next due to appear at the Old Bailey on 9 October.\nInquiries continue into other alleged genocide offences in Rwanda.\nRelated topics\nRwandan genocide\nMore on this story\nDoctor in court to face Rwanda genocide charges\nPublished\n6 days ago",
