   "title_cn": "卢旺达艺术博物馆举办大屠杀记忆主题展览",
   "title_original": "https://www.newtimes.co.rw/rssFeed/17",
   "summary_cn": "卢旺达艺术博物馆于9月22日开幕名为“What the Ground Remembers / Ibyo Ubutaka Bwibuka”的展览，持续至2027年1月20日。展览让六名1994年针对图西族大屠杀的亲历者参与决定其记忆的呈现方式，将个人证词与研究、摄影和当代艺术并列展示。展览由卢旺达艺术博物馆和卢旺达文化遗产学院等机构合作组织。",
   "summary_original": "",
   "event_time": "2026-09-29T09:24:00+08:00",
   "published_time": "2026-09-29T09:24:00+08:00",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "A new exhibition at the Rwanda Art Museum is giving people with lived experience of the 1994 Genocide against the Tutsi a direct role in deciding how their memories are presented, placing personal testimony alongside research, photography and contemporary art.\nDubbed ‘What the Ground Remembers  / Ibyo Ubutaka Bwibuka,’ the exhibition opened on September 22 and runs through January 20, 2027, bringing together six participants whose memories are tied to ordinary places across Rwanda.\nALSO READ:   Through art, East African artists explore memory, identity and inheritance\nRather than treating them only as sources for a museum project, the exhibition asks them to photograph those places, share their own words and recordings, and help shape the stories attached to them.\nThe project grew from collaborative research involving Prof. Caesar Atuire of the University of Oxford and colleagues at the University of Rwanda, including Dr. Innocent Rugaragu and Dr. Liberata Gahongayire.\nAtuire and Dr. Natalie Tegama are part of a wider research team behind the exhibition.\nALSO READ:   Rwanda’s philosophy of heroism and the resurgence of praise poetry\nThe collaboration was highlighted at the exhibition's opening by Parfait Ishungure, the Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Youth and Arts, who described the research partnership between the two universities as important to the project's approach to storytelling and history.\nIshungure said the exhibition demonstrates how a museum can become a space for greater public participation by allowing people whose lives are being interpreted to have a say in how those experiences are presented.\nThat approach is visible throughout the exhibition.\nALSO READ:   How do we archive the present before it disappears?\nThe six participants, Pierre Ndungutse, Isaac Hakizimana, Chantal Murekatete, Belise Umutoni, Claudine Dusenge and Hussein Rongomongo, took researchers to places that carry personal meaning for them.\nThose locations are not necessarily memorial sites. They include compounds, paths, market corners and other everyday spaces that have acquired significance through what happened there.\nParticipants photographed those places as they look today. The images are displayed alongside older photographs of the same locations, allowing visitors to see how the physical landscape has changed over time while considering what memories have remained.\nThose photographs and the participants' words also became the basis for painted portraits created with multimedia artist Timothy Wandulu.\nThe exhibition also includes new works by artists Benjamin Rusagara and Bless Aine Hirwa, while selected works from the Rwanda Art Museum's permanent collection are placed alongside the new pieces.\nAt the center of the exhibition is a listening space where visitors can hear recordings from the participants in their own voices. The organisers say the recordings have been left without outside interpretation so that visitors encounter the testimonies directly.\nOne of the recorded voices is Rongomongo, who has publicly acknowledged his role in the killings of 1994.\nAccording to the exhibition brief, his testimony was included after discussions with survivors involved in the project, who considered acknowledgment of harm an important part of Rwanda's experience with Gacaca and community reconciliation.\nHis account is presented alongside, rather than against, the experiences of survivors.\nThe exhibition forms part of TheMuseumsLab, a platform focused on joint learning and professional exchange around the future of museums in Africa and Europe. Museum professionals and experts from different institutions were in Kigali for a week of exchanges connected to the programme.\nIshungure said the exhibition also points to a wider question about who holds authority inside museums. He argued that display involves interpretation, and that allowing participants to act as artists and curators of their own memories changes the relationship between cultural institutions and the communities they serve.\nFor the organisers, the method leaves room for different forms of remembrance to exist together. Rwanda's   annual Kwibuka   commemoration provides a national framework for remembering the genocide, while the exhibition focuses on quieter memories attached to particular people and places.\nThe project was organised through the Rwanda Art Museum and the Rwanda Cultural Heritage Academy in collaboration with the Ministry of Youth and Arts, TheMuseumsLab, ANTITHESIS, a research platform at the University of Oxford, and Culture&.\nDr. Natalie Tegama and Vivaldi Ngenzi curated the exhibition, while Nelson Niyakire served as coordinator.\n‘What the Ground Remembers’  is open daily from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. at the Rwanda Art Museum, Kanombe.\nTue, 29 Sep 2026 00:54:30 +0200\nThere has been growing conversation in Rwanda about the rising cost and availability of beverages. My interest became practical when I went to buy a box of Inyange mineral water and found it harder to obtain than usual. A retailer later told me Petit Mutzig was out of stock, \"zarabuze,\" the same explanation I had heard weeks earlier about Amstel.\nThree products, three situations, one question: where does scarcity actually begin? A production constraint, a demand spike, reduced distributor allocations, a logistics bottleneck, or intermediaries holding stock in anticipation of shortages? The answer may differ by product, but what we see on the shelf is often only the last symptom of a problem that started earlier in the chain.\nScarcity doesn't always start with the manufacturer\nThe immediate assumption is that the manufacturer cut production, but that isn't the only explanation. For Inyange, dry-season conditions have been cited: water is not just a finished product, it is also a critical production input. WASAC has indicated that Kigali's daily water requirement exceeds current production, with reduced source levels driving rationing, and manufacturers whose cleaning, bottling and cooling depend on municipal water can feel that directly. I have no equivalent evidence, though, that the same factor explains the Petit Musting or Amstel shortages.\nSilence is not evidence\nWhen a manufacturer stays quiet, it is tempting to read that as confirmation of an internal problem. But companies may avoid statements to prevent panic buying, or the constraint may sit outside their control, in transport or distribution. The better approach is to trace the product through the chain rather than treat silence as proof.\nDifferent shortages, different fingerprints\nA production or input constraint tends to affect several brands sharing that input and persists even without a demand change. A distribution constraint creates a patchy picture, with one retailer stocked and another empty. A genuine demand shock shows up as fast sell-through that normalizes once the trigger passes. Speculative stockholding, harder to prove, is where intermediaries withhold supply to sell later at a higher price, making scarcity self-reinforcing. None of this can be diagnosed from an empty shelf alone.\nThe missing link: visibility\nThe chain runs Manufacturer to Distributor to Wholesaler to Retailer to Consumer, and at each stage information, inventory and pricing signals can shift. Distributors shape availability, pricing and the information flow between manufacturers and the market. When that system distorts, it can produce artificial scarcity, with the extra margin captured further down the chain rather than by the manufacturer, while consumers pay more.\nConsumers who repeatedly hit unavailable or pricier products switch, and a brand can lose market share not because quality changed but because the supply chain failed to deliver consistently. Availability is part of the brand.\nWhat manufacturers can do\nManufacturers cannot control weather, transport disruptions or demand swings, but they can control how fast they detect problems and how well they communicate. Real-time vis",
