   "title_cn": "埃塞俄比亚反阿比阵营的“试错”逻辑与风险",
   "title_original": "The Error in Tsmido’s Trial and Error",
   "summary_cn": "埃塞俄比亚出现以“Tsmido”为名的反阿比·艾哈迈德政府力量组合，据报涉及提格雷人民解放阵线、法诺、奥罗莫解放军等政治与武装运动，厄立特里亚据称支持部分埃塞武装团体并促成相关接触。文章认为这些力量在意识形态上分歧严重，仅因共同对手而暂时合作，缺乏后阿比时代的政治方案。作者警告，这种“先合作、后谈判”的试错策略可能导致联邦权威严重削弱、领土战争重燃及国家分裂等后果。",
   "summary_original": "Why Ethiopia’s emerging anti-Abiy alignment cannot defer what comes next The post The Error in Tsmido’s Trial and Error appeared first on Ethiopia Insight .",
   "event_time": "2026-09-17T11:10:21Z",
   "published_time": "2026-09-17T11:10:21Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.ethiopia-insight.com/2026/09/17/the-error-in-tsmidos-trial-and-error/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-error-in-tsmidos-trial-and-error&utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-error-in-tsmidos-trial-and-error",
     "source_name": "Ethiopia Insight",
     "source_group": "eth",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Viewpoint\nThe Error in Tsmido’s Trial and Error\n17 September, 2026\nMistir Sew\nSUPPORT ETHIOPIA INSIGHT\nWhy Ethiopia’s emerging anti-Abiy alignment cannot defer what comes next\nThere is an emerging logic behind the unusual constellation of forces now associated with the name Tsmido. Political and armed movements that disagree profoundly about Ethiopia’s history, constitutional structure, territorial claims, and perhaps even its future as a single political community appear willing to explore cooperation around one immediate objective: weakening or removing the government of Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed. Recent  reporting  has linked discussions involving the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF), Fano, the Oromo Liberation Army (OLA), and other opposition actors, while Eritrea has reportedly supported some Ethiopian armed groups and facilitated contacts among forces opposed to Addis Ababa.\nThere is little evidence that these actors are converging ideologically. Their understandings of Ethiopian sovereignty, federalism, self-determination, territory, and national identity remain sharply divergent. What appears to hold the emerging alignment together is a common adversary. That may be sufficient for tactical or even military cooperation. It is far less obviously sufficient for constructing a political future.\nThe deeper problem is not simply that Tsmido has failed to articulate what comes after Abiy. It may be structurally unable to do so. The alignment appears possible precisely because its participants can temporarily bracket the questions that divide them most sharply. They can agree on whom they oppose more readily than they can agree on what Ethiopia should become. Requiring agreement on those questions before acting together might make the coalition itself impossible to sustain.\nThis is where the idea of trial and error becomes useful. Trial and error is rational under conditions of incomplete knowledge only when the possible error is tolerable. In the Ethiopian context, the trial is clear enough: combine otherwise incompatible forces, alter the balance of power, weaken the federal government, and defer the political settlement until afterward. The possible error is far more consequential: severe weakening or collapse of federal authority, competing armed movements asserting legitimacy, renewed territorial wars, expanded foreign intervention, and perhaps fragmentation of the state itself.\nThe danger is compounded because the possible error does not carry the same consequences for every participant. For some actors, fragmentation would be catastrophic. For others, a weakened Ethiopian center could create strategic opportunities. The coalition may therefore be participating in the same experiment while accepting very different consequences if the experiment goes wrong.\nDifferent Stakes\nTigrayan and Amhara actors would have to confront  competing  territorial claims. Oromo nationalist forces would have to clarify the political meaning and limits of self-determination. Fano would have to determine what guarantees it requires concerning Ethiopia’s territorial integrity and continued cohesion. The participants would need a common position on the federal system, transitional authority in Addis Ababa, control of the national army, and the status of armed movements after victory.\nThese matters go to the core political identities of the prospective participants and cannot easily be resolved through ambiguity. A meaningful compromise for one actor may require another to surrender a position it considers fundamental. This creates the central paradox of Tsmido. The coalition requires some conception of a post-Abiy order if its military cooperation is to possess coherent political meaning. Yet negotiating such an order in advance may expose contradictions so fundamental that the coalition cannot survive the negotiation. The absence of a post-Abiy plan may therefore be more than an oversight. It may be one of the conditions that makes the alliance possible.\nThe strategy is consequently more problematic than simply “fight first and negotiate later.” It may operate according to a harsher logic: cooperate now because negotiating the future now could demonstrate that continued cooperation is impossible. If so, postponement is not merely a temporary weakness. It is part of the coalition’s organizing logic.\nWar’s Logic\nThere is an even deeper difficulty with allowing war to resolve these uncertainties. One of the most influential explanations for why rational actors nevertheless go to war begins with factual uncertainty. If adversaries knew with reasonable certainty how a war would end, they would ordinarily have an incentive to negotiate around the expected outcome rather than incur enormous costs only to arrive at substantially the same distribution of power afterward. Wars occur in part because each side acts on assumptions about relative military strength, political resolve, endurance, and the reliability of allies. Put simply, wars are often fought because the parties do not actually know who will win.\nIf the eventual loser knew beforehand that it would lose, rational leadership would generally prefer a negotiated settlement reflecting that reality to reaching essentially the same result after losing thousands of people and devastating the country. War can thus become a brutal mechanism for discovering facts that politics failed to establish beforehand: who is stronger, whose forces will hold, which alliances are reliable, whose population can endure longer, and which leadership has fundamentally miscalculated.\nThe difficulty is that those propositions are tested on human beings. Every actor considering another Ethiopian war is making assumptions. The federal government makes assumptions about its military superiority and political endurance. Fano makes assumptions about its ability to withstand federal pressure and expand its influence. Tigrayan forces make assumptions about the military and political balance surrounding Tigray. The OLA makes assumptions about the resilience and potential of its movement. Eritrea makes assumptions about how far Ethiopian instability can proceed without threatening its own security. Tsmido adds another assumption: that movements pursuing profoundly different political objectives can cooperate long enough to weaken the existing order without their contradictions destroying either the alliance or the country they hope to inherit.\nSome of those assumptions will inevitably be wrong. The consequences, however, are rarely borne primarily by the political leaders who made them. They are borne by the young people sent to fight, civilians living where armies move, families displaced when territory changes hands, and communities left with the social and economic consequences.\nEthiopia has only recently experienced this on a devastating scale. The 2020–22 Tigray war produced  immense  mortality, displacement, destruction, and trauma. That experience should have altered the burden of proof for everyone contemplating another military solution. Military confidence is not enough. Political leaders must also account for the degree of uncertainty underlying their assumptions and for the consequences if those assumptions prove wrong.\nUneven Risk\nTsmido is conducting two experiments at once. The first is military: whether these forces can cooperate sufficiently to defeat or fundamentally weaken the existing government. The second is political: whether the same forces, after success, can construct an Ethiopian order that all are prepared to accept. The first determines the distribution of power. The second determines what Ethiopia becomes. Human lives are the cost of conducting both.\nMore troubling still, the first experiment may become the means of generating the information necessary to resolve the second. If the participants cannot agree beforehand on the post-Abiy balance of power, conflict itself may determine who emerges strongest, who contro",
