  {
   "event_id": "EVT_67ba5d7c1276c301",
   "country": "SS",
   "country_cn": "南苏丹",
   "country_risk_level": 4,
   "country_risk_label": "极高",
   "event_type": "armed_conflict",
   "event_severity": "medium",
   "event_status": "new",
   "title_cn": "评论：谁摧毁了南苏丹？",
   "title_original": "Opinion| Who destroyed South Sudan?",
   "summary_cn": "南苏丹独立十五周年之际，一篇评论文章反思该国自2011年独立以来的历程，指出2013年12月爆发的冲突暴露了苏丹人民解放运动内部的深刻政治分歧。文章批评政治领导失败、腐败、族群政治操纵及制度薄弱，并援引前新闻部长Barnaba Marial Benjamin的说法称，大部分被盗资金发生在“durra”高粱采购丑闻期间。文章认为南苏丹的衰落是多年治理不善与错失机会逐渐累积的结果。",
   "summary_original": "Introduction Fifteen years after independence, South Sudan stands at a critical crossroads. The outbreak of The post Opinion| Who destroyed South Sudan? appeared first on Radio Tamazuj .",
   "event_time": "2026-09-30T07:19:01Z",
   "published_time": "2026-09-30T07:19:01Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/opinion-who-destroyed-south-sudan",
     "source_name": "Radio Tamazuj",
     "source_group": "ssd",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Introduction\nFifteen years after independence, South Sudan stands at a critical crossroads. The outbreak of conflict on December 15, 2013 exposed deep political divisions within the Sudan People’s Liberation Movement (SPLM) and marked the beginning of one of the darkest chapters in the country’s history. During the liberation struggle, South Sudanese were united by a common aspiration: to build a nation founded on freedom, justice, human rights, the rule of law, and people-centered development.\nAccording to South Sudan’s former Information Minister Barnaba Marial Benjamin, more than half of the stolen funds were taken during the “durra” scandal, when a large government purchase of sorghum, the country’s staple food, was never distributed.\nWhen independence was achieved on July 9, 2011, many citizens believed that decades of suffering had finally given birth to a nation that would guarantee peace, dignity, and equal opportunities for all. However, the promise of independence gradually gave way to political rivalries, corruption, ethnic polarization, and persistent instability. Instead of strengthening national unity, many political leaders increasingly prioritized personal, ethnic, and political interests above the collective aspirations of the people.\nWhat was once celebrated as the dawn of freedom and self-determination has become a painful story of conflict, economic hardship, corruption, and unfulfilled promises. Millions of South Sudanese who sacrificed for liberation envisioned a country built on justice, prosperity, and unity. Yet far too many citizens continue to endure poverty, insecurity, displacement, and uncertainty about the future.\nAs we reflect on our national journey, we must confront a difficult but necessary question:\nWho destroyed South Sudan?\nThis question is uncomfortable because it compels us to examine not only the failures of leaders but also the broader systems and decisions that have contributed to the country’s challenges. While many seek to identify a single individual or group responsible for South Sudan’s difficulties, the reality is far more complex. South Sudan’s decline has been shaped by poor leadership, corruption, weak institutions, political intolerance, ethnic manipulation, and the collective failure to safeguard the principles upon which the nation was founded. [1]\nThe country was not destroyed overnight. Rather, it has been weakened gradually through years of missed opportunities, poor governance, and the abandonment of national values that once inspired generations to struggle for freedom.\nThe failure of political leadership\nThe greatest betrayal of South Sudan has come from failures in political leadership. Independence presented a historic opportunity to build a democratic, inclusive, and prosperous nation. Instead of focusing on nation-building and institutional development, many leaders prioritized political survival, personal enrichment, and the consolidation of power.\nPublic institutions were weakened by political interference, while public resources were too often treated as private property. Political competition became a struggle for control rather than a contest of ideas, policies, and visions for national development. National interests were frequently sacrificed in pursuit of individual and factional ambitions.\nThe consequences have been devastating. The political crisis that erupted in December 2013 plunged the country into widespread violence. Thousands of people lost their lives, millions were displaced, and countless families continue to live with the trauma of conflict. The violence not only destroyed lives and livelihoods but also shattered public trust in leadership and government institutions.\nDespite several peace agreements, implementation has often been slow and inconsistent. As a result, many South Sudanese continue to question whether the country’s leaders have genuinely learned from the mistakes of the past and whether they are fully committed to lasting peace and national reconciliation.\nThe political manipulation of ethnicity\nSouth Sudan’s diversity should have been one of its greatest strengths. The country’s many ethnic communities possess rich cultures, traditions, and histories that could have formed the foundation of a strong and inclusive national identity.\nInstead, ethnic differences have too often been manipulated for political purposes. Certain political actors have exploited ethnic identities to mobilize support, spread fear, and maintain influence. This has deepened divisions and fostered mistrust among communities that once coexisted peacefully.\nLet us be honest: tribes did not destroy South Sudan. Rather, politicians and power brokers who weaponized ethnic identities for political gain contributed significantly to the country’s divisions. Ethnicity itself is not the problem. The problem arises when ethnicity is used as a tool for exclusion, domination, and political competition.\nNo nation can prosper when citizens are encouraged to view one another as rivals rather than development partners. Sustainable peace requires a shared national identity that transcends ethnic affiliations and promotes mutual respect, equality, and inclusion.\nCorruption and mismanagement of public resources\nCorruption has been one of the most destructive forces undermining South Sudan’s development. Despite possessing significant natural resources, particularly oil, the country continues to face serious levels of poverty, unemployment, and inadequate public services.\nMany citizens continue to ask a simple but important question: where have the nation’s resources gone?\nFor years, allegations of corruption and financial mismanagement have dominated public discourse. Resources that should have funded schools, hospitals, roads, and essential public services have often failed to translate into tangible improvements in the lives of ordinary citizens.\nCorruption is more than an economic crime. It is a direct assault on human dignity and social justice. Every public dollar that is stolen or mismanaged represents a child denied an education, a patient denied healthcare, a community denied development, and a young person denied opportunity.\nThe failure to address corruption effectively has weakened public confidence in government institutions and undermined trust between citizens and the state.\nThe militarization of politics\nAnother major factor contributing to South Sudan’s challenges has been the militarization of politics. For decades, violence has often been rewarded while peaceful dialogue and democratic engagement have struggled to gain equal prominence.\nToo many political actors have viewed armed conflict as a pathway to influence, recognition, and power. This dangerous culture has normalized violence as a means of resolving political disputes.\nNo nation can achieve sustainable peace when disagreements are settled through armed confrontation rather than dialogue, negotiation, and constitutional processes. The future of South Sudan cannot be built through violence. It must be built upon democratic values, respect for human rights, accountability, and the rule of law.\nPolitical leaders must recognize that lasting stability comes from inclusive governance, not military dominance. Citizens deserve a political system where ideas compete peacefully and where conflicts are resolved through institutions rather than weapons.\nWeak institutions and the crisis of governance\nStrong nations are built upon strong institutions. Independent courts, professional civil services, transparent oversight bodies, accountable security sectors, and effective local governments are essential for sustainable development and social stability.\nUnfortunately, many institutions in South Sudan remain vulnerable to political interference, inadequate resources, and limited capacity. Weak institutions create opportunities for corruption, selective justice, and abuse of power.\nWhen institutions fail to function independently and effect",
