   "title_cn": "尼日利亚NGO呼吁媒体合作，推动生殖健康议题的伦理报道",
   "title_original": "NGO seeks media collaboration on ethical reporting of reproductive health issues",
   "summary_cn": "尼日利亚非政府组织“性暴力与性别暴力司法倡导网络”（SJN）呼吁与媒体合作，推动生殖健康议题的伦理报道。SJN全国协调人Jerome Uneje在奥绍博为记者和内容创作者举办的培训上表示，培训旨在让记者了解《安全终止妊娠（STOP）指南》，并以人权视角报道性与生殖健康权利议题。该消息来自单一来源尼日利亚通讯社（NAN）。",
   "summary_original": "NGO seeks media collaboration on ethical reporting of reproductive health issues By Moromoke Akanji A non-governmental organisation (NGO), The Sexual and Gender-Based Violence (SGBV) Justice Advocacy Network (SJN), has called for collaboration with the media… News Agency of Nigeria",
   "event_time": "2026-09-30T13:10:50Z",
   "published_time": "2026-09-30T13:10:50Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://nannews.ng/srhr/",
     "source_name": "News Agency of Nigeria",
     "source_group": "nga",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Health  NGO seeks media collaboration on ethical reporting of reproductive health issues\nBy  Nefisetu Yakubu   ·   50 minutes ago   ·  3 min read\n✓\n✓\nBy Moromoke Akanji\nA non-governmental organisation (NGO), The Sexual and Gender-Based Violence (SGBV) Justice Advocacy Network (SJN), has called for collaboration with the media to promote ethical coverage and reportage of reproductive health issues in Nigeria.\nThe National Coordinator of SJN, Ms. Jerome Uneje, made the call during a one-day training for journalists and content creators on Rights-Based Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights (SRHR) Reporting and Ethical Coverage of Reproductive Health Issues on Tuesday in Osogbo.\nUneje said the training was aimed at educating journalists on the national guidelines on the Safe Termination of Pregnancy (STOP) guidelines and promoting ethical, rights-based reporting of reproductive health issues.\nShe said the training was also designed to ensure that SRHR issues were reported through a human rights lens.\nUneje said that this was necessary to protect survivors of sexual and gender-based violence, including rape, incest, as well as those experiencing mental and physical health challenges.\nUneje urged journalists to protect survivors and adopt a rights-based approach in their reports by seeking informed consent without exerting pressure or using any form of coercion.\nShe said the guidelines were designed to promote access to safe termination of pregnancy for women who needed the service.\n“This training is also to bring on board the media and journalists to assist in promoting this document so that healthcare providers and health practitioners can use it as a guideline on the procedure for safe termination of pregnancy,” she said.\nUneje added that the guidelines would also help to change public perceptions and discourage people from resorting to unsafe termination of pregnancy.\nAlso speaking, Mr Olufemi Olusola, a facilitator at the training, said SRHR referred to the right of every person to make informed decisions about their bodies, sexuality and reproductive lives.\nOlusola said SRHR also provided information on comprehensive sexuality education applicable to all people, regardless of sex, age, disability, marital status or nationality.\nHe said the SJN initiative sought to domesticate the safe termination of pregnancy guidelines by ensuring that women understood their rights and that medical practitioners could provide appropriate care to survivors in line with the guidelines.\n“The STOP guidelines are a document on legal indications that talks about the do’s and don’ts of pregnancy termination in Nigeria.\n“The guidelines were also launched in September 2023 in the state,” he said.\nOlusola encouraged survivors of rape, incest, sexual abuse and cases involving foetal abnormalities to seek medical care at government facilities for safe termination of pregnancy, where legally indicated.\n“Many people believe that abortion is illegal in Nigeria. Because of this belief, they avoid government facilities.\n“Consequently, they end up patronising unqualified practitioners, which can lead to various life-threatening complications,” he said.\nOlusola said women with life-threatening medical conditions, including maternal heart and vascular diseases, kidney diseases, cancer and mental disorders, could seek medical advice on appropriate options, including termination of pregnancy where medically and legally indicated.\nHe added that medical practitioners could rely on the guidelines in appropriate circumstances to ensure that their actions were in line with the law.\nThe News Agency of Nigeria (NAN) reports that SJN is a coalition of seven member organisations comprising women’s rights groups, women-led enterprises, media practitioners and other key stakeholders.\nThe network works collaboratively to prevent and respond to sexual and gender-based violence, promote reproductive rights to reduce the high prevalence of maternal mortality, and advance access to justice for survivors across Nigeria.(NAN) www.nannews.ng\nMAK/VE\nEdited by Victor Adeoti\n✓\n✓\nTags:    NGO\nReproductive Health\nNefisetu Yakubu",
