   "body_extracted": "General News  NEMA moves to tackle flooding through USAR programme\nBy  Philomina Attah   ·   2 hours ago   ·  2 min read\n✓\n✓\nBy Victor Nwachukwu\nThe National Emergency Management Agency (NEMA) says it is taking proactive steps to tackle flooding through its Urban Search and Rescue (USAR) programme.\nNEMA’s Head of Operations for Abia and Imo, Mr Innocent Nweze, said this on Wednesday in Owerri, during a refresher training on the USAR programme, organised for staff members and critical stakeholders.\nNweze described the programme as “very important”, given the increasing complexity of urban environments and the growing risk of building collapse, landslides, explosions, industrial accidents, communal impasse and other urban emergencies.\nHe said that “these complexities, as well as the flooding in various communities across the country, required well-trained, properly equipped, and coordinated response teams.\n“USAR operations are among the most demanding emergency response activities.\n“They require specialised knowledge, appropriate equipment, teamwork, discipline, effective communication, and the ability to make timely decisions under extremely challenging conditions.\n“More importantly, USAR operations are about saving lives and reducing suffering when every minute matters,” Nweze said.\nAccording to him, the programme is designed to strengthen the capacity of emergency responders, and enhance inter-agency coordination.\nHe further said the programme seeks to promote the adoption of internationally recognised principles and good practices in search and rescue operations, especially in urban localities.\nHe thanked NEMA’s Director-General, Mrs Umar Zubaida, and her management team for their support.\nHe also thanked partner organisations for their effort to facilitate the implementation of the programme.\nHe further urged them to remain committed to competent, coordinated and effective USAR teams, capable of responding rapidly and safely to emergencies and saving lives.\n“Effective disaster management is a collective responsibility, and programmes, such as this, demonstrate the importance of collaboration in building resilient communities,“ Nweze said.\nThe News Agency of Nigeria (NAN) reports that the Nigeria Security and Civil Defence Corps, Federal Fire Service, and the National Inland Waterways Authority were all represented at the event.\nA major highlight of the event was a simulation exercise on how to rescue a drowning person at the popular Otammiri River. (NAN)\nEdited by Abdulfatai Beki/Sam Oditah\n✓\n✓\nPhilomina Attah",
