   "title_cn": "埃塞俄比亚首都禁止无人机飞行后传出爆炸声",
   "title_original": "Explosions heard in Ethiopia's capital after drone flights banned",
   "summary_cn": "埃塞俄比亚首都亚的斯亚贝巴夜间传出爆炸声，居民和外交消息人士称，一处位于西部军事总部附近，另一处在城市南部。爆炸发生前数小时，当局禁止在亚的斯亚贝巴上空飞行无人机；此前一周，政府军与包括提格雷地区武装在内的新联盟在首都以北爆发战斗。与反对派武装有关联的消息人士向路透社声称，爆炸是其所为的无人机袭击。官方尚未确认爆炸原因、具体地点及是否有伤亡或损失。",
   "summary_original": "It comes a week after fighting erupted north of the capital between the army and a new alliance of armed groups.",
   "event_time": "2026-10-01T11:25:03Z",
   "published_time": "2026-10-01T11:25:03Z",
   "location": "",
   "china_related": false,
   "verification_level": "single_source",
   "verification_label_cn": "单一来源",
   "independent_source_count": 1,
   "source_links": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c3vgygpqky9zo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
     "source_name": "BBC News (Africa)",
     "source_group": "pan",
     "language": "en"
    }
   ],
   "potential_impact": "",
   "progress": "",
   "current_policy_passed": true,
   "quality_gate_passed": true,
   "legacy_migration_preserved": false,
   "legacy_visibility": true,
   "publication_reason": "Stage 3B 真实采集",
   "body_extracted": "Explosions heard in Ethiopia's capital after drone flights banned\nImage source,  Anadolu via Getty Images\nImage caption,    There have been concerns for security in the capital following the outbreak of fighting last week\nBy   Lucy Fleming   ,      BBC Africa      and    Teklemariam Bekit   ,  BBC Tigrinya\nPublished\n1 October 2026, 12:25 BST\nUpdated 24 minutes ago\nExplosions were heard overnight in Ethiopia's capital, some in the area of the military headquarters in the west and another in the south of the city, residents and diplomatic sources say.\nThe blasts came hours after drone flights were banned over Addis Ababa and a week after fighting erupted in the north between the army and a newly launched alliance of armed groups including forces from the Tigray region.\nA source linked to the opposition fighters told the Reuters news agency they were behind what they said were drone strikes.\n\"It was very loud. It was strong enough to set off car alarms,\" a resident in a southern area of the city, near the base of the secret service, told BBC Amharic.\nHe estimated that he heard the explosion in the Gotera neighbourhood some time around 00:30 local time (21:30 GMT).\nAnother resident told the BBC that he and his friends were playing games when they heard an explosion.\nHe lives in the western suburb of Torhayloch, a neighbourhood close to the military headquarters.\nThere were no official reports confirming the cause of the explosions, the exact locations affected or whether there were any casualties or damage.\nThe office of Ethiopia's Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed, the defence ministry as well as the federal and Addis Ababa police have not responded to BBC requests for comment.\nMeanwhile, Ethiopia's foreign ministry has said it is closing its embassy in neighbouring Eritrea and expelling 10 Eritrean diplomats \"for involvement in activities that pose a direct threat to Ethiopia's national security\".\nEarlier, in a BBC interview, Eritrea's government denied that it was backing the new rebel alliance.\nAre Ethiopians marching back to war in Tigray?\nPublished\n5 days ago\nWATCH: Why are Ethiopia's rebels joining forces?\nPublished\n3 days ago\nClashes broke out last week after months of tension between the government and the party that controls Tigray, the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF).\nThey both accuse each other of violating a 2022 peace deal that ended a brutal two-year civil war, which according to an Africa Union mediator is estimated to have cost 600,000 lives.\nLast month, seven armed movements, including the TPLF and an Amhara militia known as Fano, formed an alliance with the stated aim of removing the government in Addis Ababa.\nThere is disagreement over which side started the latest fighting.\nThe TPLF says it was responding to a federal offensive, including drone strikes, while the government says Tigrayan forces attacked first after seizing control of airports in the region.\nThe BBC is unable to report from Tigray and surrounding areas.\nIt is difficult to verify what is happening on the ground and access to the internet and the main telecoms service provider in Tigray have also been cut.\nWithin Tigray, the national army and a pro-government militia appear to be advancing towards the region's main city of Mekelle.\nIn the southern Tigrayan town of Alamata,  a medic told the BBC on Wednesday that 52 civilians had died in three days of heavy fighting .\nThe TPLF is also reported to be fighting in neighbouring Afar region, a strategic area which connects landlocked Ethiopia to Djibouti and its ports.\nA humanitarian source has estimated that tens of thousands have fled their homes in Afar, according to the AFP news agency.\nFighting around Lalibela in the Amhara region, a Unesco world heritage site where foreign tourists have been stranded for more than a week, has involved the national army and Fano fighters, now allied to the TPLF.\nThe government has accused neighbouring Eritrea of backing these groups.\nOne pro-government militia commander fighting in Tigray told the BBC that Eritrean soldiers were involved on the front lines.\nBut in a rare response to the BBC from Eritrea's government, Information Minister Yemane Gebremeskel dismissed the allegations.\nHe accused Ethiopia's ruling Prosperity Party (PP) of seeking external scapegoats for the country's mounting challenges.\n\"The PP regime has made it a habit to apportion blame to its neighbours in order to deflect attention from the myriad internal conflicts that it is fomenting in various parts of the country,\" he told BBC Tigrinya.\nEritrea was a key ally of Ethiopia during the Tigray war, fighting against the TPLF.\nBut relations between the two neighbours have since deteriorated largely over the Ethiopian prime minister's ambition to take ownership of one of Eritrea's Red Sea ports.\nEthiopia lost access to them when Eritrea seceded in 1993.\nAbiy has even hinted at taking the port of Assab - about 60km (37 miles) from the border - by force as he sees direct access to the sea as important for Ethiopia's long-term future and economic prosperity.\nAs concerns over security mount in Ethiopia, the country's National Intelligence and Security Service (Niss) announced on Wednesday that it was imposing an indefinite ban on drone flights in Addis Ababa and surrounding areas.\nThe agency said it would not issue permits for drone operations and warned that legal action would be taken against anyone flying drones in restricted areas.\nNiss said the measure was necessary to protect national leaders, critical infrastructure and major public events.\nYou may also be interested in:\nThe young women who fought in Ethiopia's last civil war and don't want to see another one\nPublished\n6 March\n'I lost my leg on the way home from school'\nPublished\n2 November 2024\nEthiopia PM's party wins landslide as fears grow of new conflict\nPublished\n21 June\nGo to    BBCAfrica.com  ,  external    for more news from the African continent.\nFollow us on Twitter    @BBCAfrica  ,  external   , on Facebook at    BBC Africa  ,  external    or on Instagram at    bbcafrica  ,  external\nRelated topics\nAfrica\nEthiopia\nEritrea\nBBC Africa podcasts\nFocus on Africa\nThis Is Africa",
